### 4. Structural Comparison Summary

| Architectural Feature | GPU Time-Slicing (Software Level) | Multi-Instance GPU / MIG (Hardware Level) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Virtualization Layer** | Managed at the software driver level via sequential queues. | Enforced at the physical silicon/hardware block level. |
| **Execution Path** | **Sequential.** Workloads alternate and take turns. | **Parallel.** Workloads execute at the exact same moment. |
| **VRAM Memory Protection** | **None.** Shared memory space; risks noisy neighbors and OOM crashes. | **Absolute.** Dedicated memory address paths and controller blocks. |
| **Hardware Compatibility** | Universal. Works on all modern architectures (Pascal, Turing, Ampere, Hopper, Blackwell). | Restrictive. Only enterprise chips (A100, H100, B200, etc.). |
| **Maximum Density** | Extremely high (Can generate dozens or hundreds of virtual replicas). | Hard ceiling (Strictly bounded up to a maximum of 7 instances). |
| **Optimal Use Case** | Cost-effective dev environments, lightweight inference apps, low-priority tasks. | Multi-tenant production clusters, strict SLAs, mission-critical operations. |

